# WebSockets

One socket per client app, typed end to end. The backend declares events with `SocketRouter`, the type bridge in `src/types/hono/hono-ws-converter.ts` turns those declarations into `WsSocketSdk`, and the client consumes them through `socketSdk`. No topic string is written twice and nothing is wired by hand.

## How a connection works

- `src/api/client.ts` creates the socket once, at import time, next to the HTTP `client`: `new Socket({ url: \`${wsProtocol}://${wsHost}/ws\` })`. Admin creates a second one on `/ws/admin`. HyperFetch connects at startup and reconnects on its own. Never hand-roll `new WebSocket` or a retry loop.
- The auth starter binds the socket to the session in `features/auth/modules/session-socket/session-socket.ts`. Its `socket.onConnect` interceptor runs before every connection attempt (first connect, automatic and manual reconnect) and calls `users/me` first: the upgrade authenticates with the session cookie, and only an HTTP request through the auth middleware can rotate an expired access token, so without this a reconnect after an idle hour is refused with 4001 and retried every five seconds for good. A 401 or 403 from that request throws, which abandons the attempt instead of looping for a signed-out visitor. The `(private)` route guard calls `connectSessionSocket` in `onEnter` and `disconnectSessionSocket` in `onLeave` (the mobile private layout does the same from an effect), so signing in opens the socket and signing out closes it.
- Every frame is JSON `{ topic, data }`. Topics are `<prefix>/<event>` (`notification/new`, `board/op`): the prefix comes from `.socket("/notification", ...)`, the event from `.emitter()` or `.listener()`.
- The backend serves the upgrade at `/ws` (`createWsRoutes`) and calls `authenticate(headers)` in `onOpen`. `null` closes the socket with code 4001. The browser sends the session cookie with the upgrade, so socket auth reuses the HTTP session and nothing goes in the URL. The upgrade never refreshes that cookie; that is what the session-socket interceptor above is for.
- Connections live in a `ConnectionManager` per namespace (`appWsManager` for `/ws`, `adminWsManager` for `/ws/admin`), keyed by user id with the organization id alongside. That registry is what `toUser`, `toOrganization`, and `broadcast` walk. Namespaces never see each other's connections.
- `initWebSocket(app)` runs before the routes and `serveWebsockets(server)` after `serve()` in `main.ts`. Both are already there. Do not add a second `createNodeWebSocket`.

## Backend: declare events

Event definitions live in `src/sockets/<domain>/index.ts`, next to but separate from HTTP routes:

```ts
import { createSocketRouter } from "@backend/libs/websocket/socket-router";
import { wsValidate } from "@backend/libs/websocket/ws-validate";
import { z } from "zod";

export type WsNotificationPayload = { id: string; title: string; readAt: string | null };

export const notificationSockets = createSocketRouter()
  .emitter("new")<WsNotificationPayload>()
  .listener("mark-read", wsValidate(z.object({ notificationId: z.string() })), async (c) => {
    await markRead({ notificationId: c.data.notificationId, userId: c.userId });
  });
```

- `.emitter(topic)<Payload>()` is server to client. It is type-only; the push happens through `wsEmit`.
- `.listener(topic, wsValidate(schema), handler)` is client to server. The handler gets `{ userId, organizationId, data, ws }`. Keep it as thin as a route: validate, call a module function, done.
- A listener has no response channel. Anything the user needs to see comes back through an emitter or an HTTP route, and invalid input is a no-op, not an error.
- Payload types are plain serializable objects (ISO strings, never `Date`). Export them from the socket file and re-export them from `src/sockets/index.ts` so clients import them from `@hype-stack/backend`.

Register the router in `src/sockets/index.ts`, mirroring `routes/index.ts`:

```ts
const sockets = new SocketRouter({ manager: appWsManager, authenticate: authenticateWsUpgrade }).socket(
  "/notification",
  notificationSockets,
);
```

Only the root router takes `manager` and `authenticate`. `authenticate` is `(headers: Headers) => Promise<WsAuthResult | null>` and comes from the auth starter (`@backend/middleware/auth/ws-auth`). The template default returns `null` and rejects every connection until it is wired. Never accept a socket without a user id.

### Pushing from a feature

Server-side pushes go through `wsEmit` (`@backend/sockets/emit`), from the domain's `features/<domain>/websockets/emitters.ts`:

```ts
import { wsEmit } from "@backend/sockets/emit";

export const pushNotification = (userId: string, notification: NotificationRecord) =>
  wsEmit.toUser({ userId, topic: "notification/new", data: serialize(notification) });
```

`toUser`, `toUsers`, `toOrganization`, and `broadcast` are checked against every declared emitter, so a wrong topic or payload fails at compile time. Never call `appWsManager.pushToUser` directly. Call the emitter after the database write commits, from the module or mutation that owns the change, so a failed write never announces itself.

## Client: consume events

```ts
import { useEmitter, useListener } from "@hyper-fetch/react";

import { client } from "@/api/client";
import { socketSdk } from "@/api/sdk";

const incoming = useListener(socketSdk.notification.new.$listener);
incoming.onEvent(({ data }) => {
  setItems((current) => [data, ...current]);
  client.cache.invalidate(/\/notifications/);
});

const markRead = useEmitter(socketSdk.notification.markRead.$emitter);
markRead.emit({ payload: { notificationId } });
```

- A backend `.emitter()` becomes `$listener` on the client and a `.listener()` becomes `$emitter`. Kebab-case topics turn camelCase (`mark-read` is `markRead`).
- Use `socketSdk` inline, always. No re-export files, no aliases, no `useEffect` with `socket.listen`, no raw `WebSocket`.
- A socket event says something changed; the HTTP cache is still the source of truth. Update local state from the payload for instant feedback, then invalidate the matching cache keys so every `useFetch` of that data catches up.
- Keep the function that applies an event to state plain (`applyNotification(items, payload)`). That is the part worth a unit test, and it does not need a socket to run.

## Adding an event, in order

1. Declare it in `src/sockets/<domain>/index.ts` and export its payload type.
2. Register the router in `src/sockets/index.ts` and re-export the type.
3. Push it from `features/<domain>/websockets/emitters.ts`, or handle it in the listener.
4. `pnpm generate`, so the client apps see the new `WsSocketSdk`.
5. Consume it with `useListener` / `useEmitter`.

## Testing

- Backend: register a stub `WSContext` whose `send` is a spy on `appWsManager` for the test user, run the module or mutation, and assert `send` received the serialized `{ topic, data }`. That exercises the real emitter and manager instead of mocking `wsEmit`.
- Frontend: `src/testing/setup/setup.ts` replaces `WebSocket` with a mock, so importing the SDK never opens a real connection. Test event handling by calling the reducer function directly, and test the HTTP side with the usual network mocks.
